  "operative_text": "Sec. 1-3: s. 27.53, 30.15, 768.28 amended (public defender conforming; sheriffs assist private schools with guardian program; sovereign immunity definition): Section 2. Paragraph (k) of subsection (1) of section 30.15, Florida Statutes, is amended to read:\nSec. 4: s. 790.001 definitions (adds \"handgun\"): Section 4. Section 790.001, Florida Statutes, is amended to read:\nSec. 5: s. 790.01 carrying concealed weapon or firearm without a license: (1) A person is authorized to carry a concealed weapon or concealed firearm, as that term is defined in s. 790.06(1), if he or she:\nSec. 6: new s. 790.013 carry without a license: identification and prohibited locations: 790.013 Carrying of concealed weapons or concealed firearms without a license.—A person who carries a concealed\nSec. 7: s. 790.015 nonresident carry; removes reciprocity-only limit: Section 7. Section 790.015, Florida Statutes, is amended\nSec. 8-9: s. 790.052 off-duty officers; s. 790.053 open carry: 790.053 Open carrying of weapons.— (1) Except as otherwise provided by law and in subsection\nSec. 10: s. 790.06 license to carry (removes duty to carry the license): Section 10. Subsection (1), paragraphs (g) and (h) of subsection (2), paragraph (e) of subsection (4), paragraph (b) of subsection (5), paragraph (f) of subsection (6), and subsections (9), (10), (12), (13), and (16) of section 79\nSec. 11: s. 790.0655 purchase/delivery of firearms; mandatory waiting period (conforming): 790.0655 Purchase and delivery of firearms; mandatory waiting period; exceptions; penalties.—\nSec. 12: s. 790.115 firearms at school-sponsored events / school property: 790.115 Possessing or discharging weapons or firearms at a\nSec. 13: repeals s. 790.145 (firearms in pharmacies): Section 13. Section 790.145, Florida Statutes, is repealed.\nSec. 14-16: s. 790.25 private conveyance carry; s. 790.251 employer/employee; s. 790.31 armor-piercing ammunition definition: 790.25 Lawful ownership, possession, and use of firearms and other weapons.—\nSec. 17: s. 943.03 FDLE conforming: 943.03 Department of Law Enforcement.—\nSec. 18: new s. 943.6873 active assailant response policy for every law enforcement agency: 943.6873 Active assailant response policy.—For the protection of all persons in this state, it is necessary and\nSec. 19-20, 22-24, 27: Office of Safe Schools, behavioral threat management process and threat management portal, threat management teams, student records, zero tolerance reporting; emergency rules: 1001.212 ... Effective upon becoming a law, subsections (14) through (17) of section 1001.212, Florida Statutes, are renumbered\nSec. 21 and 25: private-school safe school officers; safe-school officers at each public school: 1006.12 Safe-school officers at each public school.—For the protection and safety of school personnel, property,\nSec. 26: new s. 1006.121 Florida Safe Schools Canine Program: 1006.121 Florida Safe Schools Canine Program.— (1) CREATION AND PURPOSE.—\nSec. 28-36: conforming changes to s. 790.1612, 810.095 (trespass on school property with firearm), 921.0022, 921.0024 (Criminal Punishment Code), 943.051, 943.0585, 943.059, 985.11, 1002.33: 810.095 Trespass on school property with firearm or other weapon prohibited.—\nSec. 37-42: appropriations FY 2023-2024 (grant program for local law enforcement agencies; FTE positions; Office of Safe Schools; school hardening grants $42 million, others): For the 2023-2024 fiscal year, the sum of $42 million in nonrecurring funds from the General Revenue Fund is appropriated to the Department of Education\nSec. 43: effective date: Except as otherwise expressly provided in this act and except for this section, which shall take effect upon this act becoming a law, this act shall take effect July 1, 2023.",
